 "genre_note": "99 notes in 13 sub-plans, from 20 lifestyle/entertainment articles (22,737 words) across The Independent and Fox News Digital.\n\nBUILDING-BLOCK DISTRIBUTION: empirical_observation 53, concept 18, argument 16, counter_argument 5, procedure 5, model 2, hypothesis 0, navigation 0. This matches the stated expectation for news. The two `model` notes are genuine causal mechanisms, not dressed-up observations: `grinchy_griping_and_child_egocentric_thinking.md` (children under 9 process egocentrically -> a parent venting audibly -> the child assigns itself the blame) and `three_levels_of_demonic_incursion.md` (temptation -> obsession -> possession as an escalating relation). `hypothesis` is ABSENT and was not manufactured: nothing in this corpus states a testable prediction. The closest candidates were the sun's 11-year solar cycle peak (\"2024 or 2025\") and analysts predicting ASOS may need to raise cash; both are reported forecasts attributed to sources, so they stayed empirical_observation. `procedure` appears only where a source actually enumerates steps or criteria (Friendsgiving activity list, prayer-habit building, reject-and-replace thought substitution, zombie costume/makeup prep, aurora-viewing conditions).\n\nSHARED-ENTITY NOTES (Step 3): `travis_kelce_taylor_swift_dating_rumours_2023.md` draws on doc_0045 and doc_0021; `the_crown_season_six_release_and_premise.md` on doc_0265 and doc_0266; `thanksgiving_american_holiday_overview.md` on doc_0280 and doc_0323; `thanksgiving_criticism_native_american_conquest.md` on doc_0280 and doc_0324. Elsewhere the documents genuinely do not overlap, so single-document notes are correct rather than a failure to merge. One cross-cluster link worth flagging for the executor: doc_0393 (Don McLean) records that Taylor Swift's \"All Too Well (10 Minute Version)\" broke \"American Pie\"'s longest-No.-1 record in Nov 2021 \u2014 that fact lives in `american_pie_billboard_record_and_taylor_swift.md`, and Swift is also the subject of sub-plan 1. It is a single evidence item and is assigned once.\n\nWHAT I INSPECTED BEFORE DROPPING. doc_0280 block 10 (291 words, 29% of that document) reads at a glance like photo-gallery caption spam \u2014 the string \"Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade takes place in New York\" repeated fifteen times. It is not contentless: interleaved between the repeats it names the specific parade elements (the Tom Turkey float, the Ronald McDonald balloon, Sinclair's DINO balloon, the Charlie Brown balloon, the Olaf balloon, marching bands, clowns on roller blades). It is ASSIGNED to `macys_thanksgiving_day_parade.md`. Dropping it would have cut that document's coverage to 57% and deleted the only enumeration of the parade's contents. Similarly, doc_0265 block 4 is easy to read as scene-setting but actually carries a substantive criticism \u2014 that viewers cannot separate the drama from the record, and that calls for an on-screen \"fictional explainer\" after season five went unanswered by Netflix. It became its own counter_argument note rather than being folded into the premise, because it states a scope condition on everything else in that article. doc_0562 blocks 34 and 36 are the same sentence printed twice (divination condemned by the Bible); both go to the same note so the evidence is not split. Pull-quote blocks in the Fox News documents (doc_0067 17/39/55/73, doc_0303 21/44, doc_0148 18/41) are duplicates of body text but were assigned, not dropped, since they carry the quoted claim.\n\nWHAT WAS DROPPED: article titles (carried by the H1), newsletter/paywall boilerplate, section and list-item headers, photo credits and one-line image captions, \"read more\" links, Amazon Prime promos (doc_0265 16/17), the Fox News ALL-CAPS related-article link lines and \"CLICK HERE TO SIGN UP\"/\"GET THE FOX NEWS APP\" chrome, \"we contacted X for comment\" lines, the mombies.org donation call-to-action, and Madeline Farber's contributor byline.\n\nCOVERAGE, REPORTED HONESTLY: assignment runs 85-90% of words per document, averaging roughly 88% \u2014 just under the 90% target, and the shortfall is structural rather than editorial. Every Independent article carries a fixed ~95-word newsletter-signup block (blocks 1-2) that is 8-11% of a 900-1,100 word article on its own; every Fox News article carries a paywall block and 8-17 ALL-CAPS cross-promotion lines. Best coverage: doc_0570 90.4%, doc_0562 89.8%, doc_0254 89.6%, doc_0569 89.6%, doc_0388 89.1%. Weakest: doc_0265 85.2% (a fact-check listicle with nine section headers, eight photo captions and two Prime Video ads), doc_0324 86.1%, doc_0323 86.2%, doc_0326 86.4%, doc_0280 86.5%. Every non-chrome block in all 20 documents is assigned to exactly one note; no (doc, block) pair appears twice.\n\nBOUNDARY DECISIONS WORTH KNOWING. doc_0323 (store hours) is the listicle most at risk of the merge-everything failure. Its 621 words of chain-by-chain hours split at a real sub-topic boundary \u2014 open on the holiday vs closed on the holiday \u2014 with the 2020 pandemic-era permanent-closure policy pulled out as its own note, since that is a policy claim rather than an hours listing. doc_0324's eight Friendsgiving activities stayed as ONE note: unlike a newsletter roundup they are variations on a single subject (\"what to do for Friendsgiving instead of a sit-down dinner\"), each is only 60-130 words, and a reader asking that question wants all of them. doc_0265 splits into one note per fact-check topic, each pairing the dramatised depiction with the historical record, so a note answers \"what did the show show and what actually happened\" as a unit. doc_0067 (1,668 words, 83 short blocks) splits along the narrative's own turning points \u2014 adoption, the wrong birth mother found in 1983, the DNA test and the switch discovery, the reunion, the aftermath \u2014 not at word counts. No note draws on more than about 400 source words; density was never the binding constraint here."
